WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.488
Each provider names its
notices differently.

2
00:00:02.488 --> 00:00:04.858
The program sorts them into six kinds,

3
00:00:04.858 --> 00:00:09.524
because the kind decides who owns the
notice and what it asks of the agency.

4
00:00:09.524 --> 00:00:13.051
Maintenance: work is scheduled
that may affect availability,

5
00:00:13.051 --> 00:00:16.300
so check the window against
the systems that depend on it.

6
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:21.007
Retirement: a feature, version or
resource type is ending, with a date,

7
00:00:21.007 --> 00:00:24.537
so plan and approve the
migration before that date,

8
00:00:24.537 --> 00:00:28.488
and review the contingency plan
if a system depends on it.

9
00:00:28.488 --> 00:00:33.095
Advisory: information or a
recommendation, often with no deadline;

10
00:00:33.095 --> 00:00:36.613
decide whether it applies,
and record the decision.

11
00:00:36.613 --> 00:00:41.500
Security: assess the exposure and act
within the security response window.

12
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.770
Change: treat it as a proposed
change to the system,

13
00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:48.200
with an impact analysis and
then the change process.

14
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.613
And incident: something is broken now.

15
00:00:50.613 --> 00:00:52.937
Not every provider has every kind.

16
00:00:52.937 --> 00:00:57.232
Google Cloud's Personalized Service
Health reports incidents only:

17
00:00:57.232 --> 00:01:01.510
its event category has one value,
for service outage or degradation.
